mode: "100644"
text: "# The reality of research & sourcing in 2026 (verify-quarterly)\n\nThe numbers below are the case FOR verification — attribute them, and **verify-quarterly** (this field moves\nfast; the discipline doesn't).\n\n## The 2026 verification crisis (attribute)\n- **Generative search is not a source:** Columbia Journalism Review found eight generative search tools gave\n  **incorrect answers on more than 60% of tested news-citation queries.** There is no single \"hallucination\n  rate\" — reliability collapses on exactly the tasks content research needs: citation retrieval, multi-turn\n  research, niche facts (Suprmind 2026 roundup).\n- **Fabricated citations went mainstream:** the Columbia University audit published in The Lancet (May 2026)\n  scanned 2.5M biomedical papers and found fake references **surged ~12-fold since 2023** — by early 2026,\n  **1 in 277 PubMed-indexed papers** contained at least one fabricated reference (vs 1 in 2,828 in 2023). A\n  parallel 111M-reference arXiv audit found the surge beginning mid-2024, spreading into government reports,\n  legal filings, and news articles.\n- **The craft-critical failure mode — identifier hijacking:** the NeurIPS 2025 fabricated-citation taxonomy\n  found 66% of hallucinated citations were total fabrications and **29% carried working DOI links that resolve\n  to unrelated real papers.** A clickable link is NOT verification — the source must **exist AND say what's\n  claimed.**\n- **The corporate cautionary tale:** KPMG's October 2025 agentic-AI report was found to have **only 5 of 45\n  citations accurate** (GPTZero investigation via OECD.AI) — reputational damage at consultancy scale.\n- **Citogenesis in the wild:** Google's AI Overview cited an April Fool's satire (\"microscopic bees powering\n  computers\") as fact (HKS Misinformation Review) — AI now launders errors into apparent consensus, which\n  later AI answers then cite. Stanford HAI: even purpose-built legal AI hallucinated 17–34% on hard queries;\n  1,450+ court cases now involve AI hallucinations (Charlotin database). ECRI ranked AI-chatbot misuse the\n  **#1 health-technology hazard for 2026.**\n\n## The zombie-stat problem (stable craft knowledge)\nSome \"facts\" survive on repetition alone: the goldfish-attention-span claim (no traceable primary study), the\nmisapplied Mehrabian \"93% of communication is nonverbal\" (his studies measured something far narrower), and\ndecades-stale figures quoted as current. **The tell:** every citation leads to another blog post, never to a\nnamed study with a method. Ubiquity is not evidence — and one debunkable stat can discredit an otherwise\nexcellent piece, because the comment section checks.\n\n## The strategic upside (why the top 1% over-invest here)\nBeing the precisely-sourced account in a sloppy niche is a moat twice over: audiences learn whose numbers hold\n(trust compounds), and **AI answer engines preferentially cite content with verifiable sourcing** — the GEO\nflywheel `data-and-original-research` documents. Citing the primary source most competitors never read is\ndifferentiation at near-zero cost. And where no source exists, the gap is an invitation: commission the data\nand become the citation.\n\n## House metrics\nZero corrections/retractions on published claims + the source log's reuse rate + citations earned (who quotes\nyour numbers) via native signals — never fabricated.\n"
